    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `search_remote` in `kb/remote.py`. I haven't run it against a server yet. The existing tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed), but they only cover the title splitting. I also tried to check that the module imports, but running inline Python was blocked by permissions, so that check didn't run.\n\n```python\nasync def search_remote(url: str, token: str, query: str) -> list[str]:\n    headers = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\n    async with httpx2.AsyncClient(headers=headers, timeout=TIMEOUT) as http_client:\n        async with streamable_http_client(url, http_client=http_client) as (read, write):\n            async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n                await session.initialize()\n                result = await session.call_tool(\"search\", {\"query\": query})\n    return split_titles(result)\n```\n\n- **Library version:** the pinned `mcp==2.2.0` is newer than what I know, so I read its installed source instead of relying on memory. In this version, `streamable_http_client(url, http_client=...)` returns `(read, write)`. To add custom headers, the library says to pass in your own `httpx2.AsyncClient`, so that's what I did.\n- **Token on every request:** the header is set on the HTTP client itself, so every request carries it: the POSTs, the GET event stream, and the DELETE sent when the session closes. The library only follows redirects that stay on the same host, so the token isn't sent anywhere else.\n- **Timeouts:** 30s, and 300s for reads, copied from the library's own defaults. Its helper that sets these is private (`mcp.shared._httpx_utils`), so I set the values myself rather than import it.\n- **Titles:** it uses the same `split_titles` as `search_local`, so results come back in the same format.\n- **No new dependencies:** `httpx2` is imported directly, but it's already installed as a dependency of `mcp`, so `pyproject.toml` is unchanged.\n\nTo try it for real, run `KB_URL=... KB_TOKEN=... kb <query>` against the deployed server."
